AL MANARA BANK

Java Programming — Final OOP Assignment
Detailed Student Specification

Deliverable: A working Java console application and all source files.
Important: This document describes the banking problem and the behavior your program must provide. It does not tell
you exactly how to design your classes or which OOP technique to use. You are expected to analyze the requirements
and choose an appropriate design.
1. Assignment Goal
Al Manara Bank currently manages customer and account information manually. This causes mistakes when
employees handle different account types because each type has different rules. Your task is to build a console-based
Java application that helps a branch employee register customers, open accounts, perform transactions, search
records, and close accounts.
The program should behave like a small banking system. It must protect the data from invalid operations and should
clearly explain to the employee why an operation is rejected.
2. What You Are Expected To Demonstrate
Your solution should demonstrate that you can take a real-world problem and turn it into a working Java program. The
solution should make sensible use of Java basics, arrays, methods, classes and objects, constructors, data protection,
and other OOP features where they are useful. You are responsible for deciding how the design should be organized.
3. General Rules
• Use Java only. The application must run from the console.
• Do not use a database, GUI, Spring, or external libraries.
• Store the system data in memory using arrays. You may choose the array structure that makes sense for your
design.
• Do not ask the user to manually type customer IDs or account numbers when the system is supposed to generate
them.
• Invalid input should not crash the program.
• Keep the program organized into logical classes and methods. Avoid putting the entire application inside main().

