4. The Banking Scenario
Al Manara Bank has one branch. The branch serves many customers, and a customer may have more than one
account. The bank currently offers three account types: Savings, Current, and Fixed Deposit.
All accounts have common information such as an account number, an owner, a balance, and a status. However, the
rules for transactions are not identical. For example, a savings account cannot become negative, while a current
account may use an overdraft. A fixed deposit is locked until its maturity period.
The system must therefore make sure that the correct rules are applied every time an employee performs an operation.
The employee should not have to remember special rules manually.
5. Customers
A customer represents a person who uses the bank. When registering a customer, the employee enters:
• Full name
• National ID
• Phone number (optional)
• Customer tier
The system generates a unique customer ID. The customer ID must never be typed manually by the employee.
Customer tiers
Each customer belongs to one of three tiers:
• STANDARD: regular customer.
• SILVER: customer with a different monthly fee and interest bonus.
• GOLD: customer with a different monthly fee and interest bonus.
The exact fee and bonus values are part of your system configuration. Choose reasonable fixed values and document
them clearly in your program.
6. Account Status
Every account has one of the following states:
• ACTIVE — normal transactions are allowed.
• FROZEN — transactions are not allowed.
• CLOSED — the account has been closed and cannot be used for normal transactions.
A transaction attempted on a frozen or closed account must be rejected with a clear message.

