22. Data Storage Requirements
All information must remain available while the program is running. Use arrays as the main in-memory storage
mechanism.
You will need to decide how to organize the information. For example, you may need a collection of customers and
another collection of accounts. You may use one-dimensional arrays, arrays of objects, or another array-based
arrangement that fits your design.
The application should support a reasonable maximum capacity. Before adding a new customer or account, check
whether the storage has room. If it is full, display an appropriate message rather than causing an exception.
23. Automatic IDs
Customer IDs and account numbers must be generated by the application. They must not be entered manually by the
employee.
Use internal counters or another simple mechanism to ensure that newly created records receive unique identifiers
during the program's execution.
24. Relationships Between Records
A customer can own multiple accounts. Each account belongs to one customer. When displaying a customer's
accounts, the system must find the accounts that belong to that customer.
Think carefully about what information an account needs in order to identify its owner. The system should not depend
on the employee repeatedly typing the owner's name to connect records.
25. Program Organization
Your program should be divided into logical responsibilities. The main menu should mainly coordinate user interaction
rather than containing every business rule in one large block of code.
Use methods for repeated operations and for tasks that have a clear responsibility. Keep validation, searching,
calculations, and display operations organized so that the program remains readable.
26. Design Challenge
The requirements intentionally describe several account types that share information but behave differently. You must
decide how to represent this in Java so that the design is easy to maintain and another account type could be added
later.
Do not duplicate large amounts of identical logic simply because two account types share the same behavior. At the
same time, do not force completely different rules into one confusing method. Find a clean design based on the
requirements.

