29. Final Design Challenge
This assignment intentionally does not provide a class diagram or tell you exactly which class should inherit from which
class. You must analyze the requirements and decide how to organize your program.
There are several account types with shared information and different behaviors. There are also customers that can
own multiple accounts. Think about how you can represent these relationships cleanly while keeping the program easy
to understand and modify.
You should be able to explain your design if asked: why you created each class, what information belongs to each
object, where the business rules are handled, and how the different parts of the program communicate.
30. What Will Be Evaluated
Your program will be evaluated based on both correctness and design quality.
• Functionality: Does the program perform the required operations correctly?
• Business rules: Are Savings, Current, and Fixed Deposit rules correctly enforced?
• Data handling: Are customers and accounts stored and found correctly using arrays?
• Input handling: Does the program handle invalid input without crashing?
• Code organization: Is the program divided into sensible classes and methods?
• OOP design: Did you make appropriate design decisions based on the problem rather than putting everything into
one class?
• Readability: Is the code understandable and reasonably clean?
31. Important Note
There is no single required class diagram or one required implementation. You are being evaluated on whether your
program correctly solves the banking problem and whether your design is sensible, organized, and maintainable.
Read the requirements carefully before coding. First understand the rules, then plan your data and operations, and
finally implement and test the application.

